course: chemistry
lesson: 29
title: Plastics: useful and stubborn
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look round your kitchen: a water bottle, a lunchbox, a toy brick, the handle of a toothbrush. They feel different, but all are plastics, made of very long chain molecules called polymers, like thousands of paperclips clipped into one chain.
explain: Long chains make plastics bendy, tough and light. Pull a plastic bag slowly and it thins and stretches before it tears, because the chains slide past each other and line up.
explain: Plastics are useful everywhere, from hospitals to raincoats, but they are stubborn: they last for hundreds of years, and in the sea they break into tiny bits instead of disappearing.
explain: Most plastics are made from oil, but nature has its own chains too: rubber comes from the sap of rubber trees, and some new plastics are made from corn or sugar cane.
fact: Plastics are polymers: very long chain molecules made by linking many small molecules together.
fact: Most plastics are made from oil and natural gas.
fact: When a plastic bag is stretched, its long chains slide past each other and line up, so it thins out before it tears.
fact: The number inside a little triangle of arrows on a plastic item tells which kind of plastic it is; it helps sorting, but not every kind is recycled everywhere.
fact: Plastic lasts hundreds of years and slowly breaks into tiny pieces called microplastics, because few microbes can eat it.
fact: Sea turtles can mistake floating plastic bags for jellyfish and swallow them.
fact: Rubber is a natural polymer from the sap of rubber trees; its chains are joined by links that pull it back into shape after stretching.
fact: Many plastics soften when heated, because heat lets the chains move; burning plastic gives off poisonous fumes.
fact: The non-stick coating on frying pans is a slippery plastic that food cannot grip; it was discovered by accident in 1938.
fact: Some new plastics are made from plants such as corn or sugar cane; some of these can be composted, but only in special hot composters.
fact: Nylon was invented in the 1930s to replace silk; it was cheaper and stronger, so people queued for hours for the first nylon stockings; it is used in coats, ropes and toothbrush bristles.
fact: In hospitals, sealed plastic syringes, gloves and tubes keep medicine and tools free of germs and are used only once.
fact: Plastic itself is very useful; the big problem is plastic thrown away where it does not belong.
exercise: What do a plastic bottle, a lunchbox and a toy brick have in common? | All are made of long chain molecules | All are made from melted and cooled glass | All are made from tree sap | All are made of tiny metal bits
exercise: What are most plastics made from? | Oil and natural gas | Sand and lime | Melted rocks from volcanoes | Seaweed
exercise: Why does a plastic bag thin out when you stretch it slowly? | Its long chains slide past each other | Air slowly leaks out of its tiny holes | The heat of your hands melts it | It is made of very thin glass
exercise: What does the number in the little triangle on a yoghurt pot tell you? | Which kind of plastic it is | How many times it was recycled | How old the pot is | How many pots were made that day
exercise: What happens to a plastic bottle that ends up in the sea? | It breaks into tiny bits that stay for years | It dissolves in the salty water in about a week | Fish eat it and digest it safely | It turns into sand on the beach
exercise: Why do sea turtles swallow plastic bags? | They mistake them for jellyfish | They like the taste of plastic | They use them to keep warm | They think the bags are seaweed nests
exercise: Why does a rubber band snap back after stretching? | Links between its chains pull it back | The air trapped inside it pushes it back | It is full of tiny metal springs | The cold makes it shrink back
exercise: Where does natural rubber come from? | The sap of rubber trees | The milk of cows | Oil from deep wells | Old plastic bottles melted down
exercise: Why did the plastic toy go soft and droopy on the hot heater? | Heat let its long chains move | The heat turned it back into oil | The heater sucked the air out of it | The toy was full of melted wax
exercise: Why is burning plastic dangerous? | It gives off poisonous fumes | It makes the air far too wet | It explodes like a firework | It turns into hot glass
exercise: Why does food not stick to a non-stick pan? | Its slippery plastic coating gives food no grip | The pan is always kept a little wet on the inside | The metal pushes the food away | The pan is too hot for food to stick
exercise: How was the non-stick coating discovered? | By accident, in 1938 | By a cook in ancient Rome | By a robot in a factory | By mixing wax and sugar
exercise: What are some plant-based plastics made from? | Corn or sugar cane | Old car tyres | Sea shells | Coal and iron
exercise: Which material was invented in the 1930s to replace silk? | Nylon | Rubber | Glass | Cotton
exercise: Why are plastic syringes and gloves so useful in hospitals? | They can be sealed germ-free and used once | They are stronger than any metal tool | They heal wounds by themselves | They are the only thing doctors can afford
exercise: What is the real problem with plastic? | Plastic waste where it does not belong | Plastic is always poisonous to touch with bare hands | Plastic breaks the moment you drop it | Plastic is too heavy to carry around
test: Kai: "chuck the corn plastic cup on the compost heap in the garden, it'll rot". What's true? | Most need a special hot composter | It rots in a week on any heap | It turns into corn on the heap | It is no different from a carrot peel
test: "plastic's evil, ban all of it, even in hospitals". What would happen without plastic in hospitals? | Keeping tools germ-free would be much harder | Nothing at all would change | Medicine would work much better without it | Doctors would just quietly use plastic anyway
test: If you stretch a rubber band and let go, and then do the same with a plastic bag strip, what's different? | The band springs back, the bag stays stretched | Both spring back the same way | Both stay stretched the same way | The bag springs back, the band stays long
test: A cartoon says plastic in the sea just melts away in the salty water. What's true? | It breaks into tiny bits that last for years | It melts away within a few days in warm sea water | Salt turns plastic into sand | Fish eat it all up safely
test: Dad holds up a bottle: "number 1 in the triangle means it's been recycled once". Right? | No, the number says which kind of plastic it is | Yes, it counts how many times it was recycled | Yes, and number 7 means seven times | No, it says how many litres it holds
test: Your mate wants to burn plastic rubbish on the bonfire "to get rid of it". Why not? | It gives off poisonous fumes | It makes the whole bonfire go out | It makes the fire too cold | It is a waste of good fuel
test: Mia says plastic is made from melted sand. What's true? | Most plastic comes from oil and gas | She's right, sand melts into plastic | Plastic is made from tree bark | Plastic is made from melted metal
test: Why did people queue for hours for nylon stockings when they first came out? | Nylon was a cheap, strong new silk replacement | Nylon made people warmer than wool | Nylon stockings were free on the first day | Nylon was only sold to kings
test: "the pan's coating is so slippery, how come?" | It is a plastic that food cannot grip | It is covered in a thin coat of oil | It is made of ice that never melts | It is magnetic and pushes food away
test: If a plastic lunchbox is left on a hot car dashboard all day, what might happen? | It may soften and bend out of shape | It will turn into glass | It will get stronger and harder | It will start to rot like fruit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the molecular structure of plastics? | They consist of very long chain molecules termed polymers | They consist of individual atoms of a single metal | They consist of small spherical molecules of water | They consist of crystals of sand fused by heat
exam: Identify the principal reason for which plastic debris persists in the marine environment for prolonged periods. | Few microorganisms are capable of decomposing it | It is replenished continually by marine animals of many kinds | Salt water converts it into stone | It is dissolved and redeposited each night
exam: It is given that a numeral is printed within a triangle of arrows upon a plastic container. Ascertain the significance of the said numeral. | It identifies the type of plastic | It denotes the number of previous recyclings | It specifies the year of manufacture | It states the capacity in litres
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the combustion of plastics. | Poisonous fumes are liberated | Only pure water vapour is produced | The process is entirely free of hazard | The plastic is converted into oil
